description:
## Goal

After fz-5xp.30 makes runtime gateways truthful private extern declarations with exact physical export names, make one runtime-export manifest the closed physical ABI authority.

User code may still declare a private C extern with a runtime-owned symbol name. At the shared declaration boundary, a runtime-owned export must therefore match its exact physical name, ABI, arity, and wire signature or be rejected before any execution door runs. Other foreign libraries remain open-world.

The manifest is not a semantic contract. Source extern declarations own fz-visible types and correlations. The manifest owns only physical calling shape and JIT or interpreter registration. No compiler execution branch may select behavior from its symbol spelling.

## Evidence and scope

Current RUNTIME_SYMBOLS is partial and ABI-only. Its absence lets wrong declarations reach runtime exports through dlsym or lazy declaration, while name-intercepted fake arithmetic paths create additional door disagreement. fz-5xp.30 removes aliases and moves execution away from symbol interception first.

Then this ticket must:

- derive or otherwise make complete the one physical export inventory;
- validate ABI, fixed and variadic arity, and every wire lane at the common lowering boundary;
- ensure interpreter, JIT, and AOT use the same physical export and reject unsupported shapes consistently;
- retain one physical lookup or address registration mechanism for statically linked runtime exports;
- add all-door user-declared private-extern fixtures, including malformed runtime ABI and wire declarations;
- remove duplicate or partial checks, dlsym holes, and platform-accidental success paths.

## Causality

Blocked by fz-5xp.30 because exact runtime export names and ordinary generic execution must settle first.
